## an4.33
an4.33:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, toward evening the lion, king of beasts, emerges from his den, | T: “Monks, the lion, the king of beasts, leaves his lair in the evening.
an4.33:1.2 | S: yawns, | T: Having left his lair, he stretches himself.
an4.33:1.3 | S: surveys the four quarters, | T: Having stretched himself, he looks all around the four directions.
an4.33:1.4 | S: and roars his lion’s roar three times. | T: Having looked all around the four directions, he roars his lion’s roar three times.
an4.33:1.5 | S: Then he sets out on the hunt. | T: Having roared his lion’s roar three times, he heads out for game.
an4.33:1.6 | S: And the animals who hear the roar of the lion, king of beasts, are typically filled with fear, awe, and terror. | T: Any animals who hear the sound of the roar of the lion, the king of beasts, for the most part feel fear, terror, & fright.
an4.33:1.7 | S: They return to their lairs, be they in a hole, the water, or a wood; and the birds take to the air. | T: Those who live in holes go into their holes. Those who live in the water go into the water. Those who live in the forest go into the forest. Birds flee to the air.
an4.33:1.8 | S: Even the royal elephants, bound with strong harnesses in the villages, towns, and capital cities, break apart their bonds, and urinate and defecate in terror as they flee here and there. | T: Even royal bull elephants, bound by strong leather bonds in villages, towns, & capital cities, bursting & breaking their bonds, frightened, scattering their urine & feces, run to & fro.
an4.33:1.9 | S: That’s how powerful is the lion, king of beasts, over animals, how illustrious and mighty. | T: So powerful among animals, monks, is the lion, the king of beasts—so mighty & majestic.
an4.33:2.1 | S: In the same way, when a Realized One arises in the world—perfected, a fully awakened Buddha, accomplished in knowledge and conduct, holy, knower of the world, supreme guide for those fit for training, teacher of gods and humans, awakened, blessed—he teaches the Dhamma: | T: “In the same way, monks, when a Tathāgata appears in the world—worthy & rightly self-awakened, consummate in clear-knowing & conduct, well-gone, an expert with regard to the cosmos, unexcelled trainer of people fit to be tamed, teacher of devas & human beings, awakened, blessed—he teaches the Dhamma:
an4.33:2.2 | S: ‘Such is substantial reality, such is the origin of substantial reality, such is the cessation of substantial reality, such is the practice that leads to the cessation of substantial reality.’ | T: ‘Such is self-identification, such is the origination of self-identification, such is the cessation of self-identification, such is the practice leading to the cessation of self-identification.’
an4.33:2.3 | S: Now, there are gods who are long-lived, beautiful, and very happy, lasting long in their divine palaces. When they hear this teaching by the Realized One, they’re typically filled with fear, awe, and terror. | T: Any devas who are long-lived, beautiful, abounding in pleasure, established for a long time in high palaces, on hearing the Tathāgata’s Dhamma, for the most part feel fear, terror, & fright:
an4.33:2.4 | S: ‘Oh no! It turns out we’re impermanent, though we thought we were permanent! | T: ‘Being inconstant, it seems, we supposed we were constant! Being impermanent, we supposed we were permanent!
an4.33:2.5 | S: It turns out we don’t last, though we thought we were everlasting! | T: (none)
an4.33:2.6 | S: It turns out we’re transient, though we thought we were eternal! | T: Non-eternal, we supposed we were eternal!
an4.33:2.7 | S: It turns out that we’re impermanent, not lasting, transient, and included within substantial reality.’ | T: We—inconstant, impermanent, & non-eternal, it seems—are encompassed in self-identification.’
an4.33:2.8 | S: That’s how powerful is the Realized One in the world with its gods, how illustrious and mighty. | T: So powerful in the world with its devas, monks, is the Tathāgata—so mighty & majestic.”
an4.33:3.1 | S: The Buddha, the unrivaled Teacher | T: When the Awakened One, through direct knowledge, —the Teacher, the person with no peer
an4.33:3.2 | S: in all the world with its gods, | T: in the world with its devas—
an4.33:3.3 | S: rolls forth the Wheel of Dhamma | T: sets the Dhamma wheel rolling:
an4.33:3.4 | S: from his own insight: | T: (none)
an4.33:4.1 | S: substantial reality, its cessation, | T: the cessation of self-identification,
an4.33:4.2 | S: the origin of substantial reality, | T: & the cause of self-identification,
an4.33:4.3 | S: and the noble eightfold path | T: & the noble eightfold path,
an4.33:4.4 | S: that leads to the stilling of suffering. | T: leading to the stilling of suffering,
an4.33:5.1 | S: And then the long-lived gods, | T: long-lived devas—
an4.33:5.2 | S: so beautiful and glorious, | T: beautiful, prestigious—
an4.33:5.3 | S: are afraid and full of terror, | T: become fearful & frightened,
an4.33:5.4 | S: like the other beasts when they hear a lion. | T: like deer at a lion’s roar.
an4.33:6.1 | S: ‘We haven’t passed beyond substantial reality! | T: ‘We’re not beyond self-identification.
an4.33:6.2 | S: It turns out we’re impermanent!’ | T: It seems we’re inconstant,’
an4.33:6.3 | S: So they say when they hear the word | T: [they say,] on hearing the word
an4.33:6.4 | S: of the perfected one, free and unaffected.” | T: of the Worthy One, the one fully released, the one who is Such.
an4.33:6.5 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.136
sn35.136:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, gods and humans like sights, they love them and enjoy them. | T: “Monks, devas & human beings take pleasure in forms, delight in forms, rejoice in forms.
sn35.136:1.2 | S: But when sights perish, fade away, and cease, gods and humans live in suffering. | T: With the change, fading away, & cessation of forms, devas & human beings dwell in suffering & stress.
