## sn6.1
sn6.1:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
sn6.1:1.2 | S: At one time, when he was first awakened, the Buddha was staying in Uruvelā at the root of the goatherd’s banyan tree on the bank of the Nerañjarā River. | T: on one occasion, when the Blessed One was newly self-awakened, he was staying near Uruvelā on the bank of the Nerañjarā River, at the foot of the Goatherd’s Banyan Tree.
sn6.1:1.3 | S: Then as he was in private retreat this thought came to his mind, | T: Then, while he was alone & in seclusion, this line of thinking arose in his awareness:
sn6.1:1.4 | S: “This principle I have discovered is deep, hard to see, hard to understand, peaceful, sublime, beyond the scope of logic, subtle, comprehensible to the astute. | T: “This Dhamma that I have attained is deep, hard to see, hard to realize, peaceful, refined, beyond the scope of conjecture, subtle, to-be-experienced by the observant.
sn6.1:1.5 | S: But people like clinging, they love it and enjoy it. | T: But this generation delights in attachment, is excited by attachment, enjoys attachment.
sn6.1:1.6 | S: It’s hard for them to see this topic; that is, specific conditionality, dependent origination. | T: For a generation delighting in attachment, excited by attachment, enjoying attachment, this/that conditionality & dependent co-arising are hard to see.
sn6.1:1.7 | S: It’s also hard for them to see this topic; that is, the stilling of all activities, the letting go of all attachments, the ending of craving, fading away, cessation, extinguishment. | T: This state, too, is hard to see: the pacification of all fabrications, the relinquishing of all acquisitions, the ending of craving; dispassion; cessation; unbinding.
sn6.1:1.8 | S: And if I were to teach this principle, others might not understand me, which would be wearying and troublesome for me.” | T: And if I were to teach the Dhamma and if others would not understand me, that would be tiresome for me, troublesome for me.”
sn6.1:1.9 | S: And then these verses, which were neither supernaturally inspired, nor learned before in the past, occurred to the Buddha: | T: Just then these verses, unspoken in the past, unheard before, occurred to the Blessed One:
sn6.1:2.1 | S: “I’ve struggled hard to realize this, | T: “Enough now with teaching what only with difficulty I reached.
sn6.1:2.2 | S: there’s no need to reveal it now! | T: (none)
sn6.1:2.3 | S: Those mired in greed and hate | T: This Dhamma is not easily realized by those overcome with aversion & passion.
sn6.1:2.4 | S: can’t really understand this teaching. | T: (none)
sn6.1:3.1 | S: It goes against the stream, subtle, | T: What is abstruse, subtle, deep, hard to see, going against the flow—
sn6.1:3.2 | S: deep, obscure, and very fine. | T: (none)
sn6.1:3.3 | S: Those besotted by greed cannot see, | T: those delighting in passion,
sn6.1:3.4 | S: for they’re cloaked in a mass of darkness.” | T: cloaked in the mass of darkness, won’t see.”
sn6.1:4.1 | S: And as the Buddha reflected like this, his mind inclined to remaining passive, not to teaching the Dhamma. | T: As the Blessed One reflected thus, his mind inclined to dwelling at ease, not to teaching the Dhamma.
sn6.1:5.1 | S: Then, knowing the Buddha’s train of thought, it occurred to the divinity Sahampati, | T: Then Brahmā Sahampati, having known with his own awareness the line of thinking in the Blessed One’s awareness, thought:
sn6.1:5.2 | S: “Oh lord! The world will be lost, the world will perish! For the mind of the Realized One, the perfected one, the fully awakened Buddha, inclines to remaining passive, not to teaching the Dhamma.” | T: “The world is lost! The world is destroyed! The mind of the Tathāgata, the Arahant, the Rightly Self-awakened One inclines to dwelling at ease, not to teaching the Dhamma!”
sn6.1:5.3 | S: Then, as easily as a strong person would extend or contract their arm, he vanished from the realm of divinity and reappeared in front of the Buddha. | T: Then, just as a strong man might extend his flexed arm or flex his extended arm, Brahmā Sahampati disappeared from the Brahmā world and reappeared in front the Blessed One.
sn6.1:5.4 | S: He arranged his robe over one shoulder, knelt on his right knee, raised his joined palms toward the Buddha, and said: | T: Arranging his upper robe over one shoulder, he knelt down with his right knee on the ground, saluted the Blessed One with his hands before his heart, and said to him:
sn6.1:5.5 | S: “Sir, let the Blessed One teach the Dhamma! Let the Holy One teach the Dhamma! | T: “Lord, let the Blessed One teach the Dhamma! Let the One-Well-Gone teach the Dhamma!
sn6.1:5.6 | S: There are beings with little dust in their eyes. They’re in decline because they haven’t heard the teaching. | T: There are beings with little dust in their eyes who are falling away because they do not hear the Dhamma.
sn6.1:5.7 | S: There will be those who understand the teaching!” | T: There will be those who will understand the Dhamma.”
sn6.1:5.8 | S: That is what the divinity Sahampati said. Then he went on to say: | T: That is what Brahmā Sahampati said. Having said that, he further said this:
sn6.1:6.1 | S: “Among the Magadhans there appeared in the past | T: “In the past there appeared among the Magadhans
sn6.1:6.2 | S: an impure teaching thought up by the stained. | T: an impure Dhamma devised by the stained.
sn6.1:6.3 | S: Fling open the door to freedom from death! | T: Throw open the door to the Deathless!
sn6.1:6.4 | S: Let them hear the teaching <j>the immaculate one discovered. | T: Let them hear the Dhamma realized by the Stainless One!
sn6.1:7.1 | S: Standing high on a rocky mountain, | T: Just as one standing on a rocky crag
sn6.1:7.2 | S: you can see the people all around. | T: might see people all around below,
sn6.1:7.3 | S: In just the same way, All-seer, so intelligent, | T: so, intelligent one, with all-around vision,
sn6.1:7.4 | S: having ascended the Temple of Truth, | T: ascend the palace fashioned of Dhamma.
sn6.1:7.5 | S: rid of sorrow, look upon the people | T: Free from sorrow, behold the people
sn6.1:7.6 | S: swamped with sorrow,  <j>overcome by rebirth and old age. | T: submerged in sorrow, oppressed by birth & aging.
sn6.1:8.1 | S: Rise, hero! Victor in battle, caravan leader, | T: Rise up, hero, victor in battle! O Teacher,
sn6.1:8.2 | S: wander the world free of debt. | T: wander without debt in the world.
sn6.1:8.3 | S: Let the Blessed One teach the Dhamma! | T: Teach the Dhamma, O Blessed One:
sn6.1:8.4 | S: There will be those who understand!” | T: There will be those who will understand.”
sn6.1:9.1 | S: Then the Buddha, understanding the Divinity’s invitation, surveyed the world with the eye of a Buddha, out of his compassion for sentient beings. | T: Then the Blessed One, having understood Brahmā’s invitation, out of compassion for beings, surveyed the world with the eye of an Awakened One.
sn6.1:9.2 | S: And the Buddha saw sentient beings with little dust in their eyes, and some with much dust in their eyes; with keen faculties and with weak faculties, with good qualities and with bad qualities, easy to teach and hard to teach. And some of them lived seeing the danger in the fault to do with the next world, while others did not. | T: As he did so, he saw beings with little dust in their eyes and those with much, those with keen faculties and those with dull, those with good attributes and those with bad, those easy to teach and those hard, some of them seeing disgrace & danger in the other world.
sn6.1:9.3 | S: It’s like a pool with blue water lilies, or pink or white lotuses. Some of them sprout and grow in the water without rising above it, thriving underwater. Some of them sprout and grow in the water reaching the water’s surface. And some of them sprout and grow in the water but rise up above the water and stand with no water clinging to them. | T: Just as in a pond of blue or red or white lotuses, some lotuses—born & growing in the water—might flourish while immersed in the water, without rising up from the water; some might stand at an even level with the water; while some might rise up from the water and stand without being smeared by the water—
sn6.1:9.4 | S: In the same way, the Buddha saw sentient beings with little dust in their eyes, and some with much dust in their eyes; with keen faculties and with weak faculties, with good qualities and with bad qualities, easy to teach and hard to teach. And some of them lived seeing the danger in the fault to do with the next world, while others did not. | T: so too, surveying the world with the eye of an Awakened One, the Blessed One saw beings with little dust in their eyes and those with much, those with keen faculties and those with dull, those with good attributes and those with bad, those easy to teach and those hard, some of them seeing disgrace & danger in the other world.
sn6.1:9.5 | S: When he had seen this he replied in verse to the divinity Sahampati: | T: Having seen this, he answered Brahmā Sahampati in verse:
sn6.1:10.1 | S: “Flung open are the doors to freedom from death! | T: “Open are the doors to the deathless.
sn6.1:10.2 | S: Let those with ears to hear commit to faith. | T: Let those with ears show their conviction. Let them show their conviction.
sn6.1:10.3 | S: Thinking it would be troublesome, Divinity, <j>I did not teach | T: Perceiving trouble, O Brahmā, I did not tell people
sn6.1:10.4 | S: the sophisticated, sublime Dhamma among mankind.” | T: the refined, sublime Dhamma.”
sn6.1:11.1 | S: Then the divinity Sahampati, knowing that his request for the Buddha to teach the Dhamma had been granted, bowed and respectfully circled the Buddha, keeping him on his right, before vanishing right there. | T: “Then Brahmā Sahampati, thinking, ‘I’m the one who created the opportunity for the teaching of the Dhamma by the Blessed One!’ bowed down to me and, circling me on the right, disappeared right there.

## sn36.19
sn36.19:1.1 | S: Then the chamberlain Pañcakaṅga went up to Venerable Udāyī, bowed, sat down to one side, and asked him, | T: Then Pañcakaṅga the carpenter went to Ven. Udāyin and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to Ven. Udāyin,
sn36.19:1.2 | S: “Sir, how many feelings has the Buddha spoken of?” | T: “Venerable Udāyin, how many feelings have been described by the Blessed One?”
sn36.19:1.3 | S: “Chamberlain, the Buddha has spoken of three feelings: | T: “The Blessed One has described three feelings, householder:
sn36.19:1.4 | S: pleasant, painful, and neutral. | T: a feeling of pleasure, a feeling of pain, a feeling of neither pleasure nor pain.
sn36.19:1.5 | S: The Buddha has spoken of these three feelings.” | T: These are the three feelings described by the Blessed One.”
sn36.19:1.6 | S: When he said this, Pañcakaṅga said to Udāyī, | T: When this was said, Pañcakaṅga the carpenter said to Ven. Udāyin,
sn36.19:1.7 | S: “Sir, Udāyī, the Buddha hasn’t spoken of three feelings. | T: “No, Venerable Udāyin, the Blessed One hasn’t described three feelings,
sn36.19:1.8 | S: He’s spoken of two feelings: | T: he’s described two feelings:
sn36.19:1.9 | S: pleasant and painful. | T: a feeling of pleasure & a feeling of pain.
sn36.19:1.10 | S: The Buddha said that neutral feeling is included as a peaceful and subtle kind of pleasure.” | T: As for the feeling of neither pleasure nor pain, that has been described by the Blessed One as a peaceful, sublime pleasure.”
sn36.19:2.1 | S: For a second time, Udāyī said to him, | T: A second time…
sn36.19:2.2 | S: “The Buddha hasn’t spoken of two feelings, | T: (none)
sn36.19:2.3 | S: he’s spoken of three.” | T: (none)
sn36.19:2.4 | P: Sukhā vedanā, dukkhā vedanā, adukkhamasukhā vedanā— | T: (none)
sn36.19:2.5 | P: imā tisso vedanā vuttā bhagavatā”ti. | T: (none)
sn36.19:2.6 | S: For a second time, Pañcakaṅga said to Udāyī, | T: (none)
sn36.19:2.7 | S: “The Buddha hasn’t spoken of three feelings, | T: (none)
sn36.19:2.8 | S: he’s spoken of two.” | T: (none)
sn36.19:2.9 | P: sukhā vedanā, dukkhā vedanā. | T: (none)
sn36.19:2.10 | P: Yāyaṁ, bhante, adukkhamasukhā vedanā, santasmiṁ esā paṇīte sukhe vuttā bhagavatā”ti. | T: (none)
sn36.19:3.1 | S: And for a third time, Udāyī said to him, | T: A third time, Ven. Udāyin said to Pañcakaṅga the carpenter,
sn36.19:3.2 | S: “The Buddha hasn’t spoken of two feelings, | T: “No, householder, the Blessed One hasn’t described two feelings,
sn36.19:3.3 | S: he’s spoken of three.” | T: he’s described three feelings: a feeling of pleasure, a feeling of pain, a feeling of neither pleasure nor pain. These are the three feelings described by the Blessed One.”
sn36.19:3.4 | P: Sukhā vedanā, dukkhā vedanā, adukkhamasukhā vedanā— | T: (none)
sn36.19:3.5 | P: imā tisso vedanā vuttā bhagavatā”ti. | T: (none)
sn36.19:3.6 | S: And for a third time, Pañcakaṅga said to Udāyī, | T: A second time… A third time, Pañcakaṅga the carpenter said to Ven. Udāyin,
sn36.19:3.7 | S: “The Buddha hasn’t spoken of three feelings, | T: “No, Venerable Udāyin, the Blessed One hasn’t described three feelings,
sn36.19:3.8 | S: he’s spoken of two.” | T: he’s described two feelings:
sn36.19:3.9 | P: sukhā vedanā, dukkhā vedanā. | T: a feeling of pleasure & a feeling of pain.
sn36.19:3.10 | P: Yāyaṁ, bhante, adukkhamasukhā vedanā, santasmiṁ esā paṇīte sukhe vuttā bhagavatā”ti. | T: As for the feeling of neither pleasure nor pain, that has been described by the Blessed One as a peaceful, sublime pleasure.”
sn36.19:3.11 | S: But neither was able to persuade the other. | T: But neither was Ven. Udāyin able to convince Pañcakaṅga the carpenter, nor was Pañcakaṅga the carpenter able to convince Ven. Udāyin.
sn36.19:3.12 | S: Venerable Ānanda heard this discussion between Udāyī and Pañcakaṅga. | T: Ven. Ānanda heard of Ven. Udāyin’s conversation with Pañcakaṅga the carpenter.
sn36.19:4.1 | S: He went to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, | T: So he went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there,
sn36.19:4.2 | S: and informed the Buddha of all they had discussed. | T: he told the Blessed One the entire extent of Ven. Udāyin’s conversation with Pañcakaṅga the carpenter.
sn36.19:5.1 | S: “Ānanda, the explanation by the mendicant Udāyī, which the chamberlain Pañcakaṅga didn’t agree with, was quite correct. | T: (The Blessed One said,) “Ānanda, it was a genuine exposition that Pañcakaṅga the carpenter didn’t accept from Udāyin the monk, and it was a genuine exposition that Udāyin the monk didn’t accept from Pañcakaṅga the carpenter.
sn36.19:5.2 | S: But the explanation by Pañcakaṅga, which Udāyī didn’t agree with, was also quite correct. | T: (none)
sn36.19:5.3 | S: In one explanation I’ve spoken of two feelings. In another explanation I’ve spoken of three feelings, or five, six, eighteen, thirty-six, or a hundred and eight feelings. | T: There is the exposition whereby I have spoken of two feelings, the exposition whereby I have spoken of three feelings… five… six… eighteen… thirty-six… one hundred and eight feelings.
sn36.19:5.4 | P: Tissopi mayā vedanā vuttā pariyāyena. | T: (none)
sn36.19:5.5 | P: Pañcapi mayā vedanā vuttā pariyāyena. | T: (none)
sn36.19:5.6 | P: Chapi mayā vedanā vuttā pariyāyena. | T: (none)
sn36.19:5.7 | P: Aṭṭhārasāpi mayā vedanā vuttā pariyāyena. | T: (none)
sn36.19:5.8 | P: Chattiṁsāpi mayā vedanā vuttā pariyāyena. | T: (none)
sn36.19:5.9 | P: Aṭṭhasatampi mayā vedanā vuttā pariyāyena. | T: (none)
sn36.19:5.10 | S: I’ve explained the teaching in all these different ways. | T: “Thus I have taught the Dhamma by means of exposition.
sn36.19:5.11 | S: This being so, you can expect that those who don’t concede, approve, or agree with what has been well spoken will fight, quarrel, and dispute, continually wounding each other with barbed words. | T: When I have taught the Dhamma by means of exposition, if there are those who do not concede, allow, or approve of what has been well-spoken & well-stated by one another, it can be expected that they will dwell arguing, quarreling, & disputing, stabbing one another with weapons of the mouth.
sn36.19:5.12 | S: I’ve explained the teaching in all these different ways. | T: Thus I have taught the Dhamma by means of exposition.
sn36.19:5.13 | S: This being so, you can expect that those who do concede, approve, or agree with what has been well spoken will live in harmony, appreciating each other, without dispute, blending like milk and water, and regarding each other with kindly eyes. | T: When I have taught the Dhamma by means of exposition, if there are those who concede, allow, & approve of what has been well-spoken & well-stated by one another, it can be expected that they will dwell harmoniously, cordially, without dispute, becoming like milk mixed with water, regarding one another with affectionate eyes.
sn36.19:6.1 | S: There are these five kinds of sensual stimulation. | T: “Ānanda, there are these five strings of sensuality.
sn36.19:6.2 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
sn36.19:6.3 | S: Sights known by the eye, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. … | T: Forms cognizable via the eye—agreeable, pleasing, charming, endearing, enticing, linked with sensual desire. Sounds cognizable via the ear… Aromas cognizable via the nose… Flavors cognizable via the tongue…
sn36.19:6.4 | S: Touches known by the body, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: Tactile sensations cognizable via the body—agreeable, pleasing, charming, endearing, enticing, linked with sensual desire.
sn36.19:6.5 | S: These are the five kinds of sensual stimulation. | T: (none)
sn36.19:6.6 | S: The pleasure and happiness that arise from these five kinds of sensual stimulation is called sensual pleasure. | T: Now, whatever pleasure & joy arises in dependence on these five strings of sensuality,
sn36.19:6.7 | P: idaṁ vuccati kāmasukhaṁ. | T: that is called sensual pleasure.
sn36.19:6.8 | S: There are those who might say | T: “Though there are those who say,
sn36.19:6.9 | S: that this is the highest pleasure and happiness that sentient beings experience. | T: ‘They [i.e., beings] experience this as the highest existing pleasure & joy,’
sn36.19:6.10 | S: But I don’t grant them that. | T: I do not grant them that.
sn36.19:6.11 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn36.19:6.12 | S: Because there is another pleasure that is finer than that. | T: Because there is another pleasure more excellent than that pleasure and more sublime.
sn36.19:7.1 | S: And what is that pleasure? | T: “And which, Ānanda, is the other pleasure more excellent than that pleasure and more sublime?
sn36.19:7.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant, quite secluded from sensual pleasures, secluded from unskillful qualities, enters and remains in the first absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of seclusion, while placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: There is the case where a monk—quite secluded from sensuality, secluded from unskillful qualities—enters & remains in the first jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of seclusion, accompanied by directed thought & evaluation.
sn36.19:7.3 | S: This is a pleasure that is finer than that. | T: This is the other pleasure more excellent than that pleasure and more sublime.
sn36.19:7.4 | S: There are those who might say | T: “Though there are those who say,
sn36.19:7.5 | S: that this is the highest pleasure and happiness that sentient beings experience. | T: ‘They experience this as the highest existing pleasure & joy,’
sn36.19:7.6 | S: But I don’t grant them that. | T: I do not grant them that.
sn36.19:7.7 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
